ntf(b, \"```\\n%s\\n```\\n\\n\", pe.Stderr)\n```\n\nIf a failing agent ever prints ` ``` ` in its stderr (e.g., quoting markdown in an error message), the fence terminates early and the dump becomes garbled. Defensive fix: scan `pe.Stderr` for the longest run of backticks `n` and use a fence of `n+1`, or fall back to `~~~~`. Likely a non-issue today, but it's a one-line hardening.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\nCommonMark allows arbitrary-length fences (3+ backticks or tildes) precisely so you can quote content containing fences. The pattern `strings.Repeat(\"\\x60\", maxBackticksIn(s)+1)` makes user-content fencing robust.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n### Medium — Narrow type-classification leaks back into the same bug class\n\n`writeFailureHeader` only special-cases `*ProcessError`. If a future error type carries multi-line context (a parser error with a snippet, an orchestrator error with multiple agent details), it falls back to inline backticks and the original UX bug returns. Two options:\n\n1. Keep narrow today, add a one-line TODO if/when a second multi-line type appears.\n2. Switch the heuristic to \"if `runErr.Error()` contains `\\n`, fence it\":\n   ```go\n   if msg := runErr.Error(); strings.Contains(msg, \"\\n\") {\n       fmt.Fprintf(b, \"**Failed:**\\n\\n```\\n%s\\n```\\n\\n\", msg)\n       return\n   }\n   ```\n   Slightly less informative (no per-field formatting), but bug-class-resistant.\n\nI'd lean (1) — current scope is right; flag the constraint, don't pre-design.\n\n### Low — Comment verbosity\n\nThe project has been actively trimming verbose comments (`adf542e21 Trim verbose comments`), and your memory has explicit feedback that comments should justify *why* in 1–2 lines. Both new comments lean long:\n\n`dump.go:97-102` (6 lines for `writeFailureHeader`):\n> Could collapse to: `// *ProcessError.Stderr renders in a fenced block; multi-line stderr in an inline-code span loses newlines under mark